 "footnotes": [
  {
   "id": "footnote-2",
   "kind": "book",
   "number": "2",
   "html": "This is tantamount to assuming that all decay goes into a fourth state outside of the system we're considering."
  },
  {
   "id": "footnote-3",
   "kind": "book",
   "number": "3",
   "html": "<p>Formally, we are seeking a solution to the vector differential equation</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq-7-54\">$$\\dot{v} = M(\\kt/\\ktau)v(\\kt), \\tag{7.54}$$</div><p>which we guess is of the form $v(\\kt) = v^{(0)} + v^{(1)} + \\ldots$, where $v^{(0)}$ satisfies $M(\\kt/\\ktau)v^{(0)} = 0$. Substituting this result into the differential equation above, we find that</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq-7-55\">$$\\dot{v}^{(0)} \\approx M(\\kt/\\ktau)\\left(v^{(0)} + v^{(1)}\\right), \\tag{7.55}$$</div><p>or $v^{(1)} = M^{-1}(\\kt/\\ktau)\\dot{v}^{(0)}$. Such an expansion fails for the system we are considering, however, because $M$ is singular and cannot be inverted. Nonetheless, we see that the expansion does tell us some useful information about how the higher-order correction scales. To properly solve this system using some kind of <span data-concept=\"adiabatic-expansion\">adiabatic expansion</span>, one should <span data-concept=\"adiabatic-elimination\">adiabatically eliminate</span> the excited state, but solve the resulting equations of motion for $\\kcinst_{1,2}$ exactly.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "footnote-4",
   "kind": "book",
   "number": "4",
   "html": "Note that our choice of $\\kOmegaone(\\kt), \\kOmegatwo(\\kt)$ as sines and cosines has led us to a constant non-adiabatic coupling, which makes the mathematics easier. Other functions would also lead to $\\kOmegaNA$, but in general it would be a function of time."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-psi-paren",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book prints $|\\kpsi(\\kt))\\rangle$, with a doubled closing parenthesis; the state is $|\\kpsi(\\kt)\\rangle$."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-c2-2",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book prints $\\kctwo^{(2)}$. The equation is one relation for the two first-order corrections $\\kcone^{(1)}$ and $\\kctwo^{(1)}$, as the sentence goes on to say."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-eq-7-56",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book prints the integral without $d\\kt$ and with the factor $\\kgammaonethree$. With $\\dot{\\kcthree} = -\\kgammaonethree\\kcthree$ of Eq. (7.43), the norm of the state falls at the rate $2\\kgammaonethree|\\kcthree|^2$, so $\\kPjump = 2\\kgammaonethree\\int_0^{\\ktau}|\\kcthree|^2 d\\kt = 2\\kgammaonethree/\\kOmega^2\\ktau$, with $|\\kcthree| = 1/\\kOmega\\ktau$ from Eq. (7.51). The condition (7.57) is unchanged."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-final-time",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book writes $\\kOmega\\ktau = \\pi/2$. The final state is reached at $\\kt = (\\pi/2)\\ktau$, where $\\kOmegaone = \\kOmega$ and $\\kOmegatwo = 0$, whatever $\\kOmega\\ktau$ is."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-alzetta",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The paper is G. Alzetta, A. Gozzini, L. Moi and G. Orriols, Il Nuovo Cimento B 36, 5 (1976); the book misspells the first author's name and the journal."
  }
 ],
